appwrite vectorsdb update \
    --database-id <DATABASE_ID> \
    --name <NAME>
```
{% /multicode %}

# Point-in-time recovery {% #pitr %}
A dedicated VectorsDB database records its changes continuously, so you can restore it to any moment inside a retention window, for example the second before a bad migration ran. Point-in-time recovery (PITR) needs a dedicated specification; a serverless database ignores these parameters.

| Parameter | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `pitr` | Turns point-in-time recovery on or off. Defaults to `true` on `create` with a dedicated specification. |
| `pitrRetentionDays` | How many days of changes to keep, from 1 to 35 and up to your plan's maximum. Defaults to 7. Recovery points older than this are deleted. |

Pass both on `create` to choose them up front, or on `update` to change them later. A database created through the API with a dedicated specification and no `pitr` starts with PITR on and 7 days of retention; the Console's create wizard leaves PITR off unless you turn it on.

`update` writes `name` and `enabled` on every call, and `enabled` defaults to `true`, so pass the database's current values alongside the PITR parameters. Leaving `enabled` out turns a disabled database back on.

A PITR change reloads the database's configuration, so the database keeps serving and is never restarted. In the Console, open the database's **Settings** and select **Point-in-time recovery**.

PITR is billed as a share of the specification price. `listSpecifications` returns that share as `pitrRate`.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1') // Your API Endpoint
    .setProject('<YOUR_PROJECT_ID>') // Your project ID
    .setKey('<YOUR_API_KEY>'); // Your secret API key

const vectorsDB = new sdk.VectorsDB(client);

const result = await vectorsDB.update({
    databaseId: '<DATABASE_ID>',
    name: '<NAME>',
    enabled: true,
    pitr: true,
    pitrRetentionDays: 14
});
```
```python
